"""Flute (concert, C foot) as a performer: a measured additive air-jet voice, played a whole line at a time.

One note at a time. A note that starts before the previous one ends is SLURRED (no new tongue: the fingers move
the pitch under continuous air); a gap means a new tongued note; a gap of breath_gap seconds or more is a breath
between phrases (the player inhales, audibly when the param inhale is up). Every technique is a lane (automation
'inst.lane.<name>', per sample) or a param with a default, so a part is written once and played differently:

  bend      semitones added to the pitch (lip bends, rolling the flute in or out)
  vib       vibrato depth in cents (breath vibrato: pitch AND air pressure move together; it grows into held notes
            after vib_delay over vib_grow seconds, wanders in rate and depth, short notes stay straight)
  level     dB of BLOWING on the whole part: louder is brighter and less airy, softer darker and breathier,
            as on the real flute (the measured tables below), and the pitch rises a little with the air
  breath    0..1 air in the tone (multiplies the measured air; 0.5 = the measured level)
  tongue    0..1 attack at each tongued note: 0 a breath attack ('ha', air first, the tone grows out of it),
            0.6 a normal 'tu', 1 a hard 'ti' with a chiff
  scoop     semitones the note starts BELOW its pitch and rises from (read at the note's start); 0 = none
  fall      semitones the note drops at its end, fading out (read at the note's end); 0 = none
  flutter   0..1 flutter tongue (a rolled R in the air jet)

Velocity is blowing: 127 ff, 80 mf, 40 pp of the Iowa sample set the tone is fitted to (each note's measured
harmonics and air per band at pp, mf and ff, interpolated by the blowing level, so a crescendo brightens and a
diminuendo goes breathy). The register is in the tables too: the low octave has strong H2-H4 and more air, the
top octave is nearly a sine with a bright air hiss.

Measured from the University of Iowa MIS flute samples, the Philharmonia flute samples and solo flute recordings
in the public domain or under CC licences (INFO['sources']). Only numbers are kept here, no source audio. Built
and judged round by round in a winds song study (blind picture rounds, a blind ear A/B in a mix), then promoted to
the library (ledger:M321). Window rules (skills/ismail/references/windows.md): random draws keyed on song
milliseconds, causal filters, held notes arrive cut and play from where they are.
"""
